diff --git a/skills/trailer-maker/SKILL.md b/skills/trailer-maker/SKILL.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c06bae8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/skills/trailer-maker/SKILL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+---
+name: trailer-maker
+description: Cuts a game trailer from real gameplay footage. Writes a beat sheet, captures or collects clips, builds a JSON edit list, renders it with ffmpeg (bundled renderer, one command), scores it with ElevenLabs music (a composition plan timed to the beats), VO and SFX, then has two reviewers check the cut and the mix before REPORT.md. Use when asked for a trailer, teaser, launch/announce video, store-page video, a gameplay montage or a short clip for social media, even if the word "trailer" isn't used.
+---
+
+# trailer-maker
+
+Make a 30–90 s trailer from the game's own footage. Everything lives in one folder `T` (default `Trailer/<date>/`, outside `Assets/`): `beats.md`, `footage/`, `cards/`, `audio/`, `edit.json`, `out/`, `REPORT.md`. `<skills>` = the folder holding these skills; the renderer is `python3 <skills>/trailer-maker/scripts/trailer.py`.
+
+## 0. Tools
+
+Run `trailer.py check`. It finds ffmpeg (`$TRAILER_FFMPEG`, PATH, Homebrew) and lists its filters and encoders.
+Title cards are made
+in Unity (with your game's font), so the slim Homebrew formula is enough; `ffmpeg-full` adds `drawtext`/libass if
+you want text burned in by ffmpeg. No ffmpeg: ask the user to `brew install ffmpeg`; don't continue without it.
+
+## 1. Beat sheet (`T/beats.md`)
+
+Ask for length, platform (store page 16:9, social 9:16), and the one feeling the trailer sells. Write beats with times: hook (0–3 s, the most striking moment), premise, 3–5 escalating gameplay beats, a pause, the climax, title card, call to action (release date / wishlist). Every beat names the footage it needs. Get the beat sheet approved before capturing.
+
+## 2. Footage (`T/footage/`)
+
+Prefer existing recordings the user points to. Otherwise capture in Play Mode: Unity Recorder (Movie, MP4 H.264, 1920×1080 or 1080×1920, 60 fps, constant frame rate), scripted input from play-verify for repeatable shots, UI hidden unless the beat is about UI. Unity Recorder in the Editor **doesn't
+capture its audio**: record game audio separately or rebuild it in the mix (references/audio.md).
+Run `trailer.py probe T/footage/*` and note duration, size and fps of every file.
+
+## 3. Cards (`T/cards/`)
+
+Title, date and call-to-action cards are PNGs at the trailer's size: a Unity UI canvas with the game's font and logo, captured with a screenshot, so the type matches the game. Use ffmpeg `drawtext` only when `check` lists it and the user doesn't care about the font.
+
+## 4. Edit list (`T/edit.json`)
+
+One clip per beat, `in`/`out` in seconds of the source, cards with a `duration`. Short cuts (0.5–2 s) for energy, longer holds for the pause and climax; cut on action and on music hits (the beat times). Format: the docstring of `trailer.py`. Validate with `trailer.py render T/edit.json --dry-run`, then render: `trailer.py render T/edit.json`. The output under `T/out/` becomes an item in the session.
+
+## 5. Sound (`T/audio/`)
+
+ElevenLabs tools (ask before spending credits; the app confirms large ones):
+- **Music**: `music` with a `composition_plan` whose
+sections match the beat sheet, VO via `tts`, SFX via `sound_effect`; copy files to `T/audio/`, record items in
+`audio/sources.json`. Ask for 2–3 `variations` of the music and play them to the user
+for approval. Reuse items instead of regenerating; record what you used in `audio/sources.json`:
+`[{"file": "audio/music.mp3", "item": "itm_…", "tool": "music", "prompt_or_plan": …}]`.
+A licensed track works the same way from step 3 (record the license in REPORT.md).
+
+Then add the tracks to `edit.json` `audio` (music at 0, VO and SFX at their beat times), set gains so VO sits over the music, and render again. Mixing rules: references/audio.md.
+
+## 6. Review
+
+Run the two reviewers as subagents, each with only the rendered file, `beats.md` and `edit.json`: `agents/cut-reviewer.md` (pacing, clarity, story) and `agents/audio-reviewer.md` (mix, loudness, sync). Fix what they flag, render again, and review again until both pass or only taste notes are left (at most 3 rounds).
+
+## 7. Report (`T/REPORT.md`)
+
+Final file and length, beat sheet vs. cut, sources (footage, cards, audio items, licenses), the reviewers' verdicts, and what is left for the user (e.g. real game audio, localization). Never overwrite a previous render: bump `out/trailer_v2.mp4`.
+
+## Rules
+
+- Only real footage of the game; no generated video presented as gameplay.
+- Don't spend ElevenLabs credits before the beat sheet is approved.
+- Don't edit `Assets/` for the trailer except a throwaway capture scene the user agrees to.
